[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m Each of the two Magellan telescopes has a diameter of $6.5 \mathrm{~m}$. In one configuration the effective focal length is $72 \mathrm{~m}$. Find the diameter of the image of a planet (in $\mathrm{cm}$ ) at this focus if the angular diameter of the planet at the time of the observation is $45^{\prime \prime}$.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we need to use the formula for the angular size of an object and the magnification formula for telescopes.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m 1. **Angular Diameter Calculation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    The angular diameter of the planet is given as \(45''\) (arcseconds). We need to convert this to radians because the angular size formula uses radians. There are \(3600''\) in a degree and \(180\) degrees in a radian, so:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    45'' = 45 \times \frac{1}{3600} \text{ degrees} = \frac{45}{3600} \text{ degrees} = \frac{1}{80} \text{ degrees}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    Converting degrees to radians:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \theta = \frac{1}{80} \times \frac{\pi}{180} = \frac{\pi}{14400} \text{ radians}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    The angular diameter \(\theta\) of the planet is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \theta = \frac{d}{f}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    where \(d\) is the diameter of the planet and \(f\) is the focal length of the telescope. Here, \(f = 72 \text{ m}\). So:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \frac{\pi}{14400} = \frac{d}{72}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    Solving for \(d\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    d = 72 \times \frac{\pi}{14400} = \frac{72\pi}{14400} = \frac{\pi}{200} \text{ m}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    Converting meters to centimeters:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    d = \frac{\pi}{200} \times 100 = \frac{\pi}{2} \text{ cm}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m 2. **Magnification Calculation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    The magnification \(M\) of a telescope is given by the ratio of the focal length of the telescope to the diameter of the telescope:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    M = \frac{f}{D}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    where \(f\) is the focal length and \(D\) is the diameter of the telescope. Here, \(f = 72 \text{ m}\) and \(D = 6.5 \text{ m}\). So:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    M = \frac{72}{6.5} = \frac{720}{65} = \frac{144}{13}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    The diameter of the image \(d_{\text{image}}\) is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    d_{\text{image}} = M \times d
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    Substituting the values:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    d_{\text{image}} = \frac{144}{13} \times \frac{\pi}{2} = \frac{144\pi}{26} = \frac{72\pi}{13} \text{ cm}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    Using \(\pi \approx 3.14159\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    d_{\text{image}} \approx \frac{72 \times 3.14159}{13} \approx \frac{226.19448}{13} \approx 17.39957 \text{ cm}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    Rounding to the nearest whole number, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    d_{\text{image}} \approx 17 \text{ cm}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=4058381)[0m So, the diameter of the image of the planet at the focus is \(\boxed{17}\) cm.
